Subject: CPMS I02C Canonical Document and Golden Bundle Constraint Ledger

@ [ledger_uuid] 6a0e31ab-9da5-426b-ba4b-6e6e872db874

& [Required] is closed
  + exactly five root kinds with their I02B directions and no extras
  + root schema is an object
  + absent $id and $defs in the root draft before materialization
  + $schema is absent or exactly https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema
  + insert exact stable $id and exact transitive same-direction $defs closure
  + every local reference resolves within the materialized document
  + reuse the I02B canonical Value writer; do not fork normalization
  + enforce maximum document bytes for each document and maximum bundle bytes for the unsigned payload and final envelope
  + compute all digests from exact canonical bytes with explicit domain separation where a fingerprint is claimed
  + form all five documents and bundle atomically or release none

& [GoldenPaths] are fixed
  + input_deserialize.prepare_input.schema.json
  + input_deserialize.run_input.schema.json
  + input_deserialize.verify_input.schema.json
  + output_serialize.base_response.schema.json
  + output_serialize.preparation_response.schema.json
  + public_procedure_machine_schema.bundle.json

& [ProofRequirements] are adversarial
  + deterministic repeated generation and exact golden byte equality
  + schema id and direction uniqueness, exact closure, local reference resolution, and no unused closure member
  + base and preparation raw-root equality but canonical-document inequality
  + missing extra mixed conflicting tampered stale and one-byte-drift cases fail visibly
  + every active byte ceiling fails at one below observed
  + full default and all-feature workspace gates preserve existing behavior

& [Forbidden] is explicit
  - untrusted-byte parsing or duplicate-key claims
  - instance validation, serde conformance proof, semantic rule execution, or changed-schema compatibility classification
  - provider projection, model execution, service, runtime persistence, network, semantic effect, production, hardware, or FPGA
